extend schema
  @link(url: "https://specs.apollo.dev/federation/v2.3", import: ["@key", "@external"])

"""
The actual data this subgraph owns. Not a federation entity itself (no
@key) — nothing else needs to reference an Inventory row directly, it's
only ever reached via Product.inventory below.
"""
type Inventory {
    id: ID!
    productId: ID!
    quantityAvailable: Int!
    quantityReserved: Int!
    warehouseLocation: String
    lastRestockedAt: String
}

"""
This is the canonical Apollo Federation "extend an entity you don't own"
pattern: product-service owns Product (it has the @key and the real
Query/Mutation fields for it — see product-service's schema.graphqls).
This subgraph adds ONE field to that same entity without knowing or
caring about any of Product's other fields. Router resolves `id` from
product-service first, then calls back into THIS subgraph's entity
resolver (see ProductInventoryResolver.java) with that id to fill in
`inventory` — see that class's javadoc for why this is a genuinely
different execution shape (Fetch-then-Flatten) than the parallel
independent-root-field example in FederationDemo.jsx.

Deliberately NOT "extend type Product" — Federation 2 (declared via the
@link above) drops the `extend` keyword requirement entirely; a plain
`type` declaration + @key is the correct Federation 2 syntax for
contributing fields to an entity owned elsewhere. `extend type` here
actually BREAKS startup: federation-jvm's schema parser is plain
GraphQL-Java underneath, which has no concept of federation-style
extension — "extend type X" with no local base `type X` anywhere in this
subgraph's own schema is just invalid standard GraphQL to it, throwing
"The extension 'Product' type is missing its base underlying type" at
startup. Learned this the hard way — see git history for two failed
attempts before this one.

A list, not a single Inventory: a product can have stock in MULTIPLE
warehouses at once (see infra/postgres/init.sql — the table's primary
key is its own generated id, not product_id, precisely so more than one
row per product is possible). ProductInventoryResolver.java's
@BatchMapping resolves this for many products in ONE query, not one
query per product — the actual DataLoader-equivalent fix, matching
order-service's ProductBatchLoader pattern.
"""
type Product @key(fields: "id") {
    id: ID! @external
    inventory: [Inventory!]!
}

type Query {
    "Requires product:read. Direct lookup for one product's stock across all its warehouses, bypassing the Product entity extension."
    inventoryForProduct(productId: ID!): [Inventory!]!
    """
    Requires product:read. Independent of any specific product — this is
    the query FederationDemo.jsx's DASHBOARD_QUERY adds as a genuinely
    PARALLEL third branch alongside products/orders, since it doesn't
    depend on knowing any product's id in advance.
    """
    lowStockInventory(threshold: Int = 10): [Inventory!]!
}

input CreateInventoryInput {
    productId: ID!
    quantityAvailable: Int!
    quantityReserved: Int = 0
    warehouseLocation: String!
}

input UpdateInventoryInput {
    quantityAvailable: Int
    quantityReserved: Int
    warehouseLocation: String
}

type Mutation {
    "Requires product:write — same role that gates creating/editing products themselves, since inventory management lives on the product detail page. Adds a NEW warehouse record for a product (a product can have several — see Product.inventory above)."
    createInventory(input: CreateInventoryInput!): Inventory!
    "Requires product:write. Updates an EXISTING warehouse's stock levels."
    updateInventory(id: ID!, input: UpdateInventoryInput!): Inventory!
    "Requires product:write. Removes a warehouse's stock record entirely."
    deleteInventory(id: ID!): Boolean!
}
